{"text":"Evidence supports testing richer negative supervision, but harder negatives are not uniformly better. [gSASRec’s 2024 extended abstract](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) identifies overconfidence induced by sampled BCE and motivates generalized BCE as an alternative. Its [original study](https://eprints.gla.ac.uk/301348/3/301348.pdf) also evaluates SASRec with 16-negative sampled softmax. A [2024 sampling comparison](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17276), covering random, popularity, in-batch, mixed and adaptive strategies, finds dataset-dependent tradeoffs: uniform sampling favors head-item performance; popularity-based negatives can improve balance while reducing aggregate accuracy.

For safeguards, [R²NS, WWW 2026](https://renzhaochun.github.io/assets/pdf/WWW_2026_Camera_Ready.pdf), supports uniform-negative warm-up followed by progressively harder sampling. It warns that immediate hard mining can underperform uniform sampling and discusses stochastic selection among top-ranked candidates to mitigate false negatives. The [2026 RoDPO preprint](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29259) similarly reports benefits from sampling within a dynamic top-K pool instead of always choosing the hardest item. Its evidence concerns multimodal preference optimization, so transfer to ID-only SASRec remains a hypothesis.

Practical experiment proposal: retain SASRec and compare single-negative BCE, 16/64-negative sampled softmax, and generalized BCE; then test a uniform/hard mixture after warm-up. Exclude padding, the target and observed training-history items; randomize hard selection rather than repeatedly suppressing the highest scorer. These are proposed ablations, not established gains on the four requested categories. Select by pinned macro validation HR@10, inspect each category, and treat Electronics’ 14.95% ordering-error rate as motivation rather than proof that harder sampling will help."}
